Chapter: RealShowWide

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The runner for COMPILER-41. `__real_to_text` lays the integer digits up
 from `rsp` and the fraction digits from a fixed split in the same 48-byte
 frame, so the split IS the integer region's size and it has to exceed the
 widest integer part `cvttsd2si` can deliver, which is an i64 at 19 digits.
 The split was 16. An integer part of 17 or more digits ran past it and the
 fraction stage overwrote the overrun.

 It never looked like an overflow. Digits are stored least-significant
 first, so the overrun lands on the MOST significant end and the damage
 reads as a substituted digit rather than a lost one; with no fraction to
 write, the zero-fraction path stores CCE 3 at exactly the split and turns
 that digit into '0'. Before the fix `12345678901234567.0` printed as
 `02345678901234566.0` and `123456789012345678.0` as
 `103456789012345680.0`.

 Sixteen digits and below were always correct, which is why this went
 unseen: every row below is a value the old code got right except the last
 two, and nothing in the corpus printed a Real that wide.

 Each row is reported through `show` DELIBERATELY. The sibling
 real-literal-boundary reports the same magnitudes through `real-to-int`
 precisely because the printer could not be trusted; this file is the one
 that trusts it, and it is the only thing standing between this defect and
 its return.

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("d09 : " & show 123456789.0)
    print-line-uni ("d15 : " & show 123456789012345.0)
    print-line-uni ("d16 : " & show 1234567890123456.0)
    print-line-uni ("d17 : " & show 12345678901234567.0)
    print-line-uni ("d18 : " & show 123456789012345678.0)
    print-line-uni ("frac: " & show 1.5)
    print-line-uni ("half: " & show 0.5)
  end

